 To calculate the probability that it rains on at most 2 days in June, we need to sum the probabilities of it raining on exactly 0, 1, and 2 days. We can use the binomial probability formula for each of these cases. The probability of rain on a given day is $p = \frac{1}{10}$, and the probability of no rain on a given day is $1 - p = \frac{9}{10}$. There are 30 days in June. The binomial probability formula is given by: \[
P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
\] where $n$ is the number of trials (30 days), $k$ is the number of successes (raining days), and $p$ is the probability of success (raining on a given day). So we need to calculate $P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2)$. \[
P(X = 0) = \binom{30}{0} \left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^0 \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{30} = \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{30}
\]
\[
P(X = 1) = \binom{30}{1} \left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^1 \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{29} = 30 \cdot \frac{1}{10} \cdot \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{29}
\]
\[
P(X = 2) = \binom{30}{2} \left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^2 \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{28} = \frac{30 \cdot 29}{2} \cdot \left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^2 \cdot \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{28} = 435 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^2 \cdot \left(\frac{9}{10}\right)^{28}
\] Summing these probabilities gives the desired result. \] <answer> 0.647 </answer>

